        id: 38,
        title: "Talking about language",
        theory: `
        <div class="theory-section academic-forest">
            <div class="study-zone">
                <div class="theory-block">
                    <div class="theory-header">
                        <div class="theory-letter">A</div>
                        <h3>Specialist terms / Maxsus atamalar</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="web-text glossary-list">
                        <p><strong>syntax</strong> <em>(sintaksis)</em> — grammar and word order in sentences</p>
                        <p><strong>phonology</strong> <em>(fonologiya)</em> — the sound system of a language</p>
                        <p><strong>phoneme</strong> <em>(fonema)</em> — a distinct unit of sound that distinguishes meaning</p>
                        <p><strong>lexicon</strong> <em>(leksikon)</em> — the vocabulary of a language</p>
                        <p><strong>orthography</strong> <em>(orfografiya)</em> — the writing system and spelling rules</p>
                        <p><strong>modality</strong> <em>(modallik)</em> — meanings like possibility and necessity (expressed by modal verbs)</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="theory-block">
                    <div class="theory-header">
                        <div class="theory-letter">B</div>
                        <h3>Language features / Til xususiyatlari</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="web-text glossary-list">
                        <p><strong>diphthong</strong> <em>(diftong)</em> — a sound made by combining two vowels (e.g. /eɪ/ in "day")</p>
                        <p><strong>intonation</strong> <em>(intonatsiya)</em> — the rise and fall of the voice when speaking</p>
                        <p><strong>colloquial</strong> <em>(og'zaki)</em> — informal language used in everyday conversation</p>
                        <p><strong>dialect</strong> <em>(sheva)</em> — a form of language spoken in a particular area</p>
                        <p><strong>jargon</strong> <em>(maxsus lug'at)</em> — specialised vocabulary used by a particular profession</p>
                        <p><strong>slang</strong> <em>(jargon)</em> — very informal words used by particular groups</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="theory-block">
                    <div class="theory-header">
                        <div class="theory-letter">C</div>
                        <h3>Language learning / Til o'rganish</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="theory-text-column">
                        <p class="web-text">
                            Your <strong>mother tongue</strong> <em>(ona tili)</em> is the first language you learned. A <strong>bilingual</strong> <em>(ikki tilli)</em> person speaks two languages fluently. <strong>Fluency</strong> <em>(ravonlik)</em> is the ability to speak smoothly and easily. <strong>Proficiency</strong> <em>(malaka)</em> is a high level of skill in a language. An <strong>accent</strong> <em>(talaffuz uslubi)</em> is the way you pronounce words based on where you're from.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>`,
